;;; major-theorems-table.sexp -- the MAJOR THEOREMS of the user's notes against the library.
;;; Hand-maintained (first drawn up 2026-09-21 by four mapping agents, scratchpad/triage/MAJOR-THEOREMS.md).
;;; One (entry ...) per numbered Theorem / Proposition / Lemma / Corollary of the notes, in the notes' order.
;;; Read by major-theorems.scm, which writes reference/major-theorems.tex at every load.
;;; UPDATE an entry when its theorem is restated or proven: `names', `status', `deviation'.

;;; ===== calculus-ch1-2 =====
;;; calculus-ch1-2.sexp -- MAJOR THEOREMS table, agent MT-1.
;;; Document: /home/ubuntu/docs/calculus.pdf, "Supplements to Calculus", F. Javier Thayer.
;;; Scope: CHAPTER 1 (Basic Notions) and CHAPTER 2 (Differentiation), all numbered
;;; Theorem / Proposition / Lemma / Corollary, in the order of the notes.
;;; Numbered Definitions, Remarks, Axioms and Example 2.7 are deliberately omitted.
;;; Library statements read off reference/THEOREMS.md (2026-09-21 generation).
;;;
;;; Two deviations recur across the whole of Chapter 2 and are repeated in each entry
;;; because the brief asks for each instance separately:
;;;   (D1) is-diff-at(f, a, l) requires f in fun(rr, rr), i.e. f TOTAL on RR, where the
;;;        notes define differentiability for a function defined on an interval, a
;;;        neighbourhood or a half-neighbourhood only.
;;;   (D2) The library has NO one-sided (right / left) derivative at all; every
;;;        differentiability notion in the tree is two-sided, and the continuity
;;;        hypothesis forall x in ccint(a, b). is-continuous-at(rr-ms, rr-ms, f, x)
;;;        demands TWO-SIDED continuity at the endpoints a and b.

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.1")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Order compatible with addition and multiplication on Z")
       (names rr-lt-add rr-mul-le-right rr-mul-pos)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The additive half is rr-lt-add, stated over RR (x < y and u <= v implies x + u < y + v), not over ZZ; ZZ inherits it through zz-in-rr. The multiplicative half, a < b and c in NN implies a * c < b * c, is NOT stated anywhere: rr-mul-le-right is the NON-STRICT form (a <= b and 0 <= c implies a * c <= b * c) and rr-mul-pos gives only 0 < a and 0 < b implies 0 < a * b. There is also no ZZ-specific order lemma of either shape; the notes derive both from the definition b - a in N, which the library does not use.")
       (notes-statement "a, b, c integers, a < b: then a + c < b + c; and if c in N then a * c < b * c."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.2")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Trichotomy of the integer ordering")
       (names rr-lt-trichotomy zz-trichotomy)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "rr-lt-trichotomy is stated over RR (u, v in RR implies u < v or u = v or v < u), not over ZZ; the ZZ statement in the tree, zz-trichotomy, is only the special case against 0 (a < 0 or a = 0 or 0 < a). The notes' pair form for integers has no library statement of its own.")
       (notes-statement "For all integers k, l, one of k < l, k = l, l < k holds."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.3")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "1 is the least natural number")
       (names nn-one-le-succ zz-discrete nn-pos-is-succ)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The notes' N starts at 1; VNB's NN CONTAINS 0 (axiom nn-zero-in), so forall c in NN. 1 <= c is FALSE in the library and no theorem states it. The nearest are nn-one-le-succ (forall n in NN. 1 <= succ(n)) and zz-discrete (a in ZZ and 0 < a implies 1 <= a), i.e. the statement for the POSITIVE elements only. The notes derive this from the least-member axiom for subsets of N; the library's counterpart, nn-least-element, is proven.")
       (notes-statement "For every c in N, 1 <= c."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.4")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "No integer strictly between m and m + 1")
       (names zz-lt-succ-le zz-discrete)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The library states the contrapositive form zz-lt-succ-le (a, b in ZZ and a < b implies a + 1 <= b) rather than the negated-existence form of the notes; the two are interderivable in one step. No statement is phrased with the term m + 1 as an upper bound.")
       (notes-statement "If m in Z, there is no k in Z with m < k < m + 1."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.9")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of the least upper bound")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing states that two least upper bounds of a subset of RR coincide. The library takes sup as a total functoid governed by the three axioms rr-sup-in, rr-sup-upper, rr-sup-least (all three stamped asserted, warrant: reference), so uniqueness is built into the notation rather than proved. The nearest statements are esup-unique, which is the same claim for the EXTENDED nonnegative reals rr-pos-star with bounds quantified over rr-pos-star, and the axiom rr-leq-antisymmetric from which the notes' one-line proof would run.")
       (notes-statement "For any set S, least upper bounds a, a' for S are identical."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.11")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The nested interval property")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No statement about a nested sequence of closed real intervals ccint(a_n, b_n) with non-empty intersection. The nearest is nested-closed-balls-point, which is the corresponding fact in a COMPLETE metric space for closed BALLS and carries the extra hypothesis that the radii satisfy rds(succ(n)) <= recip(n + 1), so it is not the interval statement; also intersection-of-membership and heine-borel-ccint / compact-iff-fip, from which the interval statement would follow with work. Theorem 1.19 below, which the notes prove FROM 1.11, is itself proven in the library by a different route.")
       (notes-statement "If the closed intervals [a_n, b_n] are nested, i.e. [a_(n+1), b_(n+1)] is contained in [a_n, b_n] for all n in N, then the intersection over k in N of [a_k, b_k] is non-empty."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.13")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Order isomorphism of (-1, 1) with the extended reals")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing about the map x |-> x / (1 - abs(x)), its inverse s |-> s / (1 + abs(s)), its oddness, or any order isomorphism onto RR-STAR. The extended line exists only as the membership predicate rr-star-membership (x in RR-STAR iff x in RR or x = POS-INF or x = NEG-INF) with the two order axioms pos-inf-upper-bound and neg-inf-lower-bound; there is no order-isomorphism vocabulary for it and no extension of the map to the closed interval [-1, 1].")
       (notes-statement "f: x |-> x / (1 - abs(x)) is an order preserving bijection from (-1, 1) onto R; its inverse is g: s |-> s / (1 + abs(s)); f(-x) = -f(x); and f extends to an order preserving bijection from [-1, 1] onto the extended real line."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.17")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of limits")
       (names rr-limit-unique metric-limit-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "")
       (notes-statement "If x_n is a sequence of reals with lim_n x_n = a and lim_n x_n = b, then a = b."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "1.19")
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Bolzano-Weierstrass on a closed interval")
       (names rr-interval-seq-has-convergent-subseq rr-bolzano-weierstrass)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "rr-interval-seq-has-convergent-subseq types the sequence as fun(nn, ccint(lov, hiv)) and asserts a strictly monotone phi and a limit ptv in RR; like the notes' statement it does NOT claim the limit lies in [a, b], although the notes' proof produces such a limit. It also carries no a < b guard (vacuous when the interval is empty). rr-bolzano-weierstrass is the companion for a sequence merely bounded in absolute value.")
       (notes-statement "Let a, b in R. Any sequence x_k of elements of the closed interval [a, b] has a convergent subsequence."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.1")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Derivative of the monomial x^n")
       (names deriv-power deriv-const)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "deriv-power states the case n >= 1 in the shifted form is-diff-at(vnb-lambda(x, rr, x^succ(n)), a, succ(n) * a^n) for n in NN; the n = 0 case of the notes is a separate theorem, deriv-const. The notes also assert one-sided differentiability of x^n on [a, b) and (a, b] as part of the proof; the library has no one-sided notion (D2) and its is-diff-at requires the monomial as a TOTAL function on RR (D1), which it is.")
       (notes-statement "For n a non-negative integer and f(x) = x^n on R: f is everywhere differentiable and f'(x) = n x^(n-1)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.3")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Higher derivatives of x^n")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No statement computing nth-deriv of a monomial, and no notion of infinite differentiability. The tree has nth-deriv only through its two defining equations nth-deriv-zero and nth-deriv-succ and through the Taylor development; the nearest computational fact is deriv-power for the single derivative and deriv-polynomial for the derivative of a partial-sum polynomial.")
       (notes-statement "For any integer n, f(x) = x^n is infinitely differentiable and for any integer k the k-th derivative is k! x^(n-k) if k <= n and 0 if k > n."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.4")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Differentiability implies continuity")
       (names diff-implies-continuous diff-on-implies-continuous)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The notes state the ONE-SIDED form (right differentiable at x implies right continuous at x, and likewise on the left); the library has no one-sided derivative and no one-sided continuity (D2), so diff-implies-continuous is the two-sided statement only, and it requires f total on RR (D1). diff-on-implies-continuous is the same fact for is-diff-on over a normed field, where f in fun(u, carr(k)) with u OPEN, so it still does not reach a half-neighbourhood.")
       (notes-statement "If f is right (respectively left) differentiable at x, then f is right (respectively left) continuous at x."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.5")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Derivative of a sum")
       (names deriv-sum diff-on-sum)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The notes state the ONE-SIDED form; the library has only the two-sided derivative (D2). deriv-sum requires f and g total on RR (D1) and concludes about the term vnb-lambda(x, rr, f(x) + g(x)). diff-on-sum is the same law for is-diff-on over a normed field k with U open, which covers the notes' domain hypothesis for the two-sided case but not the one-sided one.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are right differentiable (respectively left differentiable) at a, then f + g is right differentiable at a and D+(f + g)(a) = D+f(a) + D+g(a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.6")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Derivative of a product")
       (names deriv-product diff-on-product)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The notes state the ONE-SIDED form; the library has only the two-sided derivative (D2). deriv-product requires f and g total on RR (D1). diff-on-product is the same law for is-diff-on over a normed field k with U open. Note the notes give no hypothesis on g beyond right differentiability at x, and neither does the library.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are right differentiable at x then f * g is right differentiable at x and D+(f * g)(x) = D+f(x) g(x) + f(x) D+g(x)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.8")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Chain Rule")
       (names deriv-chain diff-on-chain deriv-chain-value)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "deriv-chain requires f and g to be TOTAL on RR (D1), where the notes ask only that f be defined in a neighbourhood U of a and g in a neighbourhood V of f(a). diff-on-chain is the version on OPEN sets over a normed field and does carry the neighbourhood hypotheses in the form is-diff-on(k, u, f, a, l), is-diff-on(k, s, g, f(a), m) plus forall y in u. f(y) in s -- an extra hypothesis the notes do not state, that f maps U into the domain of g. The library conclusion is the product m * l of scalars; the notes write the composition symbol in (13), which for real-valued functions is the same product.")
       (notes-statement "f defined in a neighborhood U of a, g defined in a neighborhood V of f(a), f differentiable at a, g differentiable at f(a): then g o f is differentiable at a and (g o f)'(a) = g'(f(a)) f'(a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.10")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Vanishing of the derivative at an interior extremum")
       (names interval-local-max-right-deriv-nonpos interval-local-max-left-deriv-nonneg interval-local-max-deriv-zero interior-max-deriv-zero)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-09-22 as the notes state it, sentence by sentence (the first three names): at a LOCAL maximum of a function ON [a, b], a right derivative is <= 0, a left derivative is >= 0, and at an interior point a derivative is 0. The one-sided derivatives are has-right-deriv-at and has-left-deriv-at (the notes' (5) and (6), in Caratheodory form on a half-window; the limit form of (5)/(6) is not stated: the library has no limit of a function given only on a set). The notes' Definition 2.9 as printed has the two inequalities exchanged; is-local-max-at uses f(x) <= f(t), the sense the notes' proof uses. The last name is the older form: f TOTAL on RR and a GLOBAL maximum on [a, b].")
       (notes-statement "f defined on [a, b] with a local maximum at x in [a, b]: if D+f(x) is defined then D+f(x) <= 0; if D-f(x) is defined then D-f(x) >= 0; in particular if x is not an endpoint and f is differentiable at x then f'(x) = 0."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.11")
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Generalized Mean Value Theorem")
       (names generalized-mvt-on-interval generalized-mvt)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-21 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name). The derivative is the local relation has-deriv-at(f, x, l), so f'(theta) appears as a value l. The second name is the older form for functions TOTAL on RR with two-sided continuity at the endpoints; it remains as the lemma behind the first.")
       (notes-statement "a < b real, f and g continuous real-valued on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b): there is theta in (a, b) with f'(theta) (g(b) - g(a)) = g'(theta) (f(b) - f(a))."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.12")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Rolle's Lemma")
       (names rolle-on-interval rolle)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-21 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name). The derivative is the local relation has-deriv-at(f, x, l), so f'(theta) appears as a value l. The second name is the older form for functions TOTAL on RR with two-sided continuity at the endpoints; it remains as the lemma behind the first.")
       (notes-statement "a < b, h a continuous real-valued function on [a, b], differentiable on (a, b), with h(b) = h(a): there is theta in (a, b) with h'(theta) = 0."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.13")
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Mean Value Theorem")
       (names mvt-on-interval mvt)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-21 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name). The derivative is the local relation has-deriv-at(f, x, l), so f'(theta) appears as a value l. The second name is the older form for functions TOTAL on RR with two-sided continuity at the endpoints; it remains as the lemma behind the first. The quotient of the notes is stored as (f(b) - f(a)) * recip(b - a): the reader desugars `/' when it parses.")
       (notes-statement "a < b real, f continuous real-valued on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b): there is theta in (a, b) with f'(theta) = (f(b) - f(a)) / (b - a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.14")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Increment bounds from derivative bounds")
       (names mvt-upper-bound mvt-lower-bound mvt-abs-bound)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Both halves of the notes are present, as two separate theorems, plus the absolute-value form mvt-abs-bound. f is required to be in fun(rr, rr), i.e. TOTAL on RR, and the continuity hypothesis is TWO-SIDED continuity at a and b; the notes ask for f continuous on [a, b]. The derivative bound is attached to the existential witness -- forsome l. is-diff-at(f, x, l) and l <= m -- rather than stated as f'(theta) <= M for every theta in (a, b); the two agree because the derivative is unique (derivative-unique).")
       (notes-statement "a < b real, f continuous on [a, b], differentiable on (a, b): if f'(theta) <= M for all theta in (a, b) then f(b) - f(a) <= M (b - a); if f'(theta) >= m for all theta in (a, b) then f(b) - f(a) >= m (b - a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.15")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Zero derivative implies constant")
       (names deriv-zero-constant-on-interval deriv-zero-implies-constant deriv-zero-const-up)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-22 for a function ON [a, b] (the first name). `f is a constant' is written as `f takes the same value at any two points of [a, b]'. The remaining names are the older forms for functions TOTAL on RR with two-sided continuity at the endpoints; they remain as lemmas.")
       (notes-statement "a < b real, f continuous on [a, b], differentiable on (a, b) with f'(theta) = 0 for all theta in (a, b): then f is constant on [a, b]."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.16")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of the interpolating Taylor polynomial at 0")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing states existence and uniqueness of a polynomial of degree n - 1 whose first n - 1 derivatives at 0 agree with those of f. The tree has the Taylor polynomial as a functoid, taylor-poly, with the unfold r8e-tp-unfold and the two agreement facts taylor-poly-at-center and taylor-center-partial-sum (the polynomial's VALUE at the centre is f of the centre); it has no statement that its derivatives at the centre match f's, and no uniqueness statement, nor any notion of the DEGREE of a polynomial function.")
       (notes-statement "If f is n - 1 times differentiable at 0, there is exactly one polynomial P of degree n - 1 whose n - 1 derivatives at 0 coincide with those of f, namely P(x) = sum over k < n of f^(k)(0) x^k / k!."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.18")
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Taylor's formula with a general auxiliary function G")
       (names taylor-formula-on-interval taylor-lagrange)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-09-22 for functions ON [a, b] with a general G, as in the notes (the first name); its conclusion gives (20) twice, multiplied through by (n-1)! and literally with the division. n is written succ(m). The derivatives f, f', ..., f^(n) enter as a finite family d(0), ..., d(n) (is-taylor-family): each d(k), k < n, continuous on [a, b] with derivative d(k+1) on (a, b); no derivative is taken at an endpoint, where the notes speak of f^(n-1) `defined on [a, b]' (a one-sided derivative at a and b). The second name is the older Lagrange form for functions TOTAL on RR.")
       (notes-statement "f a function on [a, b], n >= 1, f^(n-1) defined and continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b); R_n(a, b) defined by f(b) = sum over k < n of f^(k)(a)(b - a)^k / k! + R_n(a, b). If G is real-valued, continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), then there is xi in (a, b) with G'(xi) R_n(a, b) = f^(n)(xi) (b - xi)^(n-1) (G(b) - G(a)) / (n - 1)!."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.19")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Taylor's formula with Lagrange remainder")
       (names taylor-lagrange-on-interval taylor-lagrange)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-09-22 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name), from 2.18 with G(x) = (b - x)^n; derivatives as the finite family of 2.18. The second name is the older form for functions TOTAL on RR, with the remainder cross-multiplied by the factorial.")
       (notes-statement "Under the hypotheses of 2.18, there is xi in (a, b) with R_n(a, b) = f^(n)(xi) (b - a)^n / n!."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.20")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Supremum bound for the Taylor remainder")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No statement bounding the Taylor remainder by (b - a)^n / n! times the SUPREMUM of abs(f^(n)) over the open interval. The library has only the pointwise-witness form taylor-lagrange, and the normed-space analogues vector-taylor-remainder-bound and nvs-taylor-remainder-bound, which bound by the norm of the derivative AT THE WITNESS theta, not by a supremum; no sup over an open interval of a derivative family is formed anywhere.")
       (notes-statement "Under the hypotheses of 2.18, abs(R_n(a, b)) <= (b - a)^n / n! times sup of abs(f^(n)(xi)) over xi in (a, b)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.21")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Continuous remainder factor S_n")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing constructs a continuous function S_n on [a, b] with R_n(t) = S_n(t)(t - a)^n / n!, nothing states S_n(a) = f^(n)(a) or the bound abs(S_n(t)) <= sup over [a, b] of abs(f^(n)). The nearest machinery is the Caratheodory-style witness used for is-diff-at itself (the phi of the definition, and diff-at-witness-family), which produces a continuous factor for the FIRST-order increment only.")
       (notes-statement "f on [a, b], n >= 1, f^(n) defined and continuous on [a, b]; R_n(t) = f(t) - sum over k < n of f^(k)(a)(t - a)^k / k!. Then there is a continuous S_n on [a, b] with R_n(t) = S_n(t)(t - a)^n / n!, with R_n(a) = f^(n)(a) and abs(S_n(t)) <= sup over xi in [a, b] of abs(f^(n)(xi))."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.22")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Taylor's formula is exact for a polynomial")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No statement that a polynomial function of degree n equals its own order-n Taylor expansion about an arbitrary centre. There is no predicate for being a polynomial function nor any notion of degree; polynomial functions appear only as the explicit term vnb-lambda(x, rr, series-partial-sum(vnb-lambda(k, nn, cf(k) * x^k), succ(n))), for which deriv-polynomial and poly-antiderivative are proven. The carrier carr(poly(a)) is the ALGEBRAIC polynomial ring (finitely supported sequences), which is not connected to that function term by any theorem.")
       (notes-statement "If f is a polynomial function of degree n, then f(a + h) = sum over k <= n of f^(k)(a) h^k / k!."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.24")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Mean Value Theorem for a directional derivative")
       (names dir-deriv-mvt dir-deriv-increment)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "There is no open set U in the library statements. dir-deriv-mvt requires f in fun(vec(m), rr), i.e. f TOTAL on the normed vector space, and replaces the hypothesis that the partial derivative is defined on U by hypotheses on the SEGMENT CURVE seg-curve(m, f, a, eta): two-sided continuity at every point of ccint(0, 1), including the endpoints 0 and 1, and differentiability at every interior point. dir-deriv-increment is the closer match in shape -- it concludes f(b + zeta) - f(b) = dir-deriv(m, f, b + th * zeta, zeta) for some th in (0, 1) -- but it asks that the directional derivative in direction zeta exist at EVERY point of vec(m), where the notes ask only that it be defined on U with the segment [a, a + eta] inside U. The ambient space is an arbitrary normed vector space, not the specific E = R^n of the notes.")
       (notes-statement "U contained in E open, the partial derivative d_eta f defined on U, and the closed segment [a, a + eta] contained in U: then f(a + eta) - f(a) = d_eta f(a + theta eta) for some theta in (0, 1)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.25")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Homogeneity of the directional derivative")
       (names dir-deriv-homogeneous)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "f is required to be in fun(vec(m), rr), i.e. TOTAL on the normed vector space, where the notes ask that f and d_eta f be defined on a neighbourhood of a. In the other direction the library is MORE general: it needs only is-dir-diff-at(m, f, a, eta, dl) at the single point a, not that d_eta f be defined on a whole neighbourhood. The ambient space is an arbitrary normed vector space, not E = R^n. Neither statement guards lambda against 0, although the notes' proof divides by lambda.")
       (notes-statement "f and d_eta f defined on a neighborhood of a: for any lambda in R, d_(lambda eta) f(a) is defined and equals lambda d_eta f(a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.26")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Additivity of the directional derivative under continuity")
       (names dir-deriv-additive)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "f is required to be in fun(vec(m), rr), i.e. TOTAL on the normed vector space, and the existence hypothesis is global: forall x in vec(m). forsome k. is-dir-diff-at(m, f, x, eta, k), and likewise for xi, where the notes ask only that d_eta f and d_xi f exist in a NEIGHBOURHOOD of a. The continuity hypothesis is stated at the single point a -- is-continuous-at of the maps x |-> dir-deriv(m, f, x, eta) and x |-> dir-deriv(m, f, x, xi) at a -- where the notes ask for continuity throughout the neighbourhood. The ambient space is an arbitrary normed vector space, not E = R^n. The conclusion, that d_(eta + xi) f(a) is DEFINED and equals the sum, is carried by the relational is-dir-diff-at and so is faithful.")
       (notes-statement "f: U --> R with d_eta f(x) and d_xi f(x) existing and continuous in x in a neighborhood of a in U: then d_(eta + xi) f(a) is defined and equals d_eta f(a) + d_xi f(a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.27")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Linearity of the directional derivative on the span")
       (names dir-deriv-linear)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The notes quantify over ALL x in U and ALL phi in the vector space V generated by eta and xi, and assert that phi |-> d_phi f(x) is a linear MAP. dir-deriv-linear states only the single instance at the single point a: from is-dir-diff-at at a in directions eta and xi it concludes is-dir-diff-at(m, f, a, r*eta + s*xi, r*dl + s*dm), and it does so under extra hypotheses the notes do not carry at that level -- that the directional derivatives in directions r*eta and s*xi exist at EVERY point of vec(m) and that the two derivative maps are continuous at a. f is required total on vec(m). No span or generated-subspace vocabulary is used, and no statement says the assignment is linear as a map.")
       (notes-statement "f: U --> R with d_eta f(x), d_xi f(x) defined and continuous in x: then for all x in U and all phi in the vector space V generated by eta and xi, d_phi f(x) is defined, and for each x in U the mapping phi |-> d_phi f(x) is linear."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.28")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuous partials give a directional derivative in every direction")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The space E = R^n with the sup norm max(abs(eta_1), ..., abs(eta_n)) does not exist in the library, and neither do the unit basis vectors e_i nor the partial derivatives d_(e_i) f. The nearest facts are the two-direction lemmas dir-deriv-additive and dir-deriv-homogeneous over an abstract normed vector space, which are what an induction on k would use; the induction over a basis, the conclusion that d_eta f is linear in eta, and the conclusion that it is continuous in x are all absent.")
       (notes-statement "U contained in E, f: U --> R, and d_(e_i) f(x) for i = 1, ..., n defined and continuous as functions of x in U: then for all eta in E and x in U, d_eta f(x) is defined, is linear in eta and continuous in x."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "2.29")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuous partials imply differentiability")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing states total differentiability of a function on R^n from the continuity of its partial derivatives; there is no R^n with the sup norm, no basis vectors e_i, no Df(a) = (d_(e_1) f(a), ..., d_(e_n) f(a)), and no estimate of the form abs(f(a + eta) - f(a) - sum eta_i d_(e_i) f(a)) <= norm(eta) eps. The whole first-order differentiability theory in the tree is scalar: is-diff-at over RR and is-diff-on over a normed field. CLAUDE.md records Props 2.28 and 2.29 as the next terrain, after the metric subspace structure.")
       (notes-statement "f such that the partial derivatives d_(e_i) f(x) are defined and continuous in x in a neighborhood of a: then for any eps > 0 there is delta such that for norm(eta) <= delta, abs(f(a + eta) - f(a) - sum over i of eta_i d_(e_i) f(a)) <= norm(eta) eps."))

;;; ===== calculus-ch3-7 =====
;;; calculus-ch3-7.sexp -- MAJOR THEOREMS table, docs/calculus.pdf chapters 3-7.
;;; Mapping agent MT-2, 2026-09-21.  Format: scratchpad/triage/MAJOR-THEOREMS.md.
;;;
;;; Scope: every numbered Theorem / Proposition / Lemma / Corollary of
;;; chapter 3 (Basic Metric Space Theory), chapter 4 (Integration),
;;; chapter 5 (Spaces of Continuous Functions), chapter 6 (Analytic Functions),
;;; chapter 7 (Complex Analysis), in the order of the notes.
;;; Chapters 6 and 7 contain NO numbered Theorem, Proposition, Lemma or
;;; Corollary: chapter 6 has only Definition 6.1 and chapter 7 only
;;; Definition 7.1, so no entry is generated for them.
;;; Definitions, Remarks, Examples and Tables are excluded throughout.
;;; 33 entries.

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.3")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Unions and finite intersections of open sets")
       (names union-of-opens-open inter-of-opens-open)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The intersection half is proven only for TWO open sets (inter-of-opens-open: is-open(s,u) and is-open(s,w) implies is-open(s, intersection(u,w))); there is no statement for the intersection of a finite family, which is what the notes assert. The union half is stated for a family presented as an index class a with an index map g, forall i in a, is-open(s, g(i)) implies is-open(s, big-union(i, a, g(i))), rather than for a family of sets; both carry is-metric-space(s) as an explicit guard.")
       (notes-statement "The union of any family of open sets is open.  The intersection of finitely many open sets is open."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.7")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A monotone bounded sequence converges to its sup (resp. inf)")
       (names monotone-convergence-rr)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The library proves only that a non-decreasing sequence bounded above CONVERGES; it does not identify the limit with sup_n x_n, which is the content of the notes' statement. The non-increasing / bounded-below / inf half is absent entirely. The sequence must be typed f in fun(nn, rr), the monotonicity is given in step form forall k in nn, f(k) <= f(succ(k)), and the bound as forsome bnd in rr, forall k in nn, f(k) <= bnd.")
       (notes-statement "If {x_n} is non-decreasing and bounded above then lim_n x_n = sup_n x_n; if {x_n} is non-increasing and bounded below then lim_n x_n = inf_n x_n."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.9")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Closed sets are exactly those stable under limits of sequences")
       (names closed-limit-in closed-set-limit-in closed-set-limit-in-tail)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Only the FORWARD direction is proven: if A is closed and a sequence of elements of A converges, its limit lies in A. The CONVERSE -- if every convergent sequence of elements of A converges to an element of A then A is closed -- is absent, and it is the half whose proof in the notes constructs the sequence. closed-limit-in types the sequence as f in fun(nn, a); closed-set-limit-in states membership pointwise as forall n in nn, f(n) in a; closed-set-limit-in-tail only from some index onwards.")
       (notes-statement "A set A in a metric space X is closed if and only if every sequence of elements of A which converges, converges to an element of A."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.10")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A set is closed if and only if it contains its accumulation points")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has no notion of accumulation point (cluster-point is for SEQUENCES, not for sets), and no statement of this equivalence. The nearest facts are about the closure: closure-membership (x in closure(s,a) iff x in pts(s) and every open u containing x meets a), closure-is-closed, closure-contains and closure-in-closed.")
       (notes-statement "A necessary and sufficient condition that A subset X be closed is that it contain all its accumulation points, x being an accumulation point of A when every ball B(x,r) contains an element of A distinct from x."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.12")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Four equivalent characterizations of a compact metric space")
       (names compact-iff-fip compact-iff-cluster-point compact-iff-seq-compact compact-iff-tb-complete)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "All four equivalences exist, but as four separate two-way statements each guarded by is-metric-space(s), not as one chain. The finite intersection property is relativised to the library predicate has-fip(s,c), which adds two hypotheses the notes do not make: the family c must be INHABITED (forsome a0, a0 in c), and only INHABITED finite subfamilies f are required to have a common point, so the notes' case F = empty (which forces X non-empty) is not covered. The library also proves the sequentially-compact form compact-iff-seq-compact (every sequence has a CONVERGENT SUBSEQUENCE), which the notes do not state; their clause (3) is compact-iff-cluster-point.")
       (notes-statement "For a metric space X the following are equivalent: (1) X is compact; (2) X satisfies the finite intersection property for closed sets; (3) every sequence in X has a cluster point; (4) X is totally bounded and complete."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.14")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuity at a point is sequential continuity")
       (names continuous-at-iff-sequential continuous-at-implies-sequential)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The library carries the typings as explicit antecedents -- is-metric-space(s), is-metric-space(t), f in fun(pts(s), pts(t)) and a in pts(s) -- where the notes get them from the ambient sentence f : X --> Y. The sequence is quantified over sqn(pts(s)) and the image sequence is written compose(f, sq).")
       (notes-statement "f : X --> Y is continuous at a if and only if for every sequence {x_n} of elements of X with x_n --> a one has f(x_n) --> f(a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.15")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuity equals closedness (openness) of preimages")
       (names continuous-implies-closed-preimage closed-preimage-implies-continuous continuous-implies-open-preimage open-preimage-implies-continuous)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated as four separate implications rather than as one three-way equivalence, so the notes' (2) iff (3) is not a library statement. The two converse directions carry the extra typing hypotheses is-metric-space(s), is-metric-space(t) and f in fun(pts(s), pts(t)); the forward directions read them off is-continuous(s,t,f), whose defining iff contains them.")
       (notes-statement "The following are equivalent: (1) f is continuous; (2) the inverse image f^-1(A) of any closed A subset Y is closed; (3) the inverse image f^-1(U) of any open U subset Y is open."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.16")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Subsequence principle")
       (names subsequence-principle)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Explicit typings is-metric-space(s), f in fun(nn, pts(s)) and l in pts(s) are added as antecedents, and subsequences are presented as strictly-mono-nn reindexings composed by subseq(subseq(f, phi), psi) rather than as sequences in their own right.")
       (notes-statement "If {x_n} is a sequence in X such that every subsequence of {x_n} has itself a subsequence which converges to a, then x_n --> a."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.19")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The continuous image of a compact set is compact")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "There is no theorem about the IMAGE of a compact set (or space) under a continuous map. The library's compactness transport facts all go the other way: closed-subset-of-compact-is-compact (a closed subspace of a compact space is compact), continuous-implies-open-preimage and continuous-implies-closed-preimage (preimages). A subset A subset X compact would in this library be is-compact(subspace-ms(s, a)), and no statement relates that to is-compact(subspace-ms(t, image(f, a))).")
       (notes-statement "If F : X --> Y is continuous and A subset X is compact, then f(A) is compact."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.20")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A continuous bijection from a compact space is a homeomorphism")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has no homeomorphism predicate for metric spaces; is-hom-metrizable-top-space and is-hom-top-space name only one-way continuity of preimages, not a bi-continuous bijection. Proposition 3.19, on which this corollary rests, is also absent.")
       (notes-statement "If X is compact and f : X --> Y is a continuous bijective map, then f is a homeomorphism."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.21")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A continuous function on a compact space is uniformly continuous")
       (names continuous-uniformly-continuous-on-ccint)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The general metric-space statement is absent. What exists is the single special case X = a closed bounded interval of RR, Y = RR, and it deviates in three ways: f is required to be in fun(rr, rr), i.e. TOTAL on RR, with continuity assumed at every x in ccint(a, b) and hence TWO-SIDED at the endpoints a and b; the conclusion is spelled out epsilon-delta inline (for a given eps there is d_ > 0 with abs(f(p_) - f(q_)) <= eps for p_, q_ in ccint(a, b) at distance <= d_) and does NOT use the library's own is-uniformly-continuous predicate; and the epsilon is a PARAMETER of the theorem rather than universally quantified inside it. The guard is a <= b, so the degenerate interval a = b is allowed.")
       (notes-statement "If X is compact, then any continuous function f : X --> Y is uniformly continuous."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.22")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuity plus uniform continuity off a compact set gives uniform continuity")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near it. The library has is-uniformly-continuous and is-compact but no theorem combining them beyond the two trunc-metric identity maps (trunc-metric-id-uniformly-continuous and its converse).")
       (notes-statement "Suppose X, Y are metric spaces and f : X --> Y is continuous.  If K subset X is compact and f is uniformly continuous on X \\ K, then f is uniformly continuous."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.23")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A continuous function constant outside a compact set is uniformly continuous")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Absent, as is Proposition 3.22 from which the notes derive it.")
       (notes-statement "If f : X --> Y is continuous and constant outside a compact set K, then f is uniformly continuous."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.26")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Unique uniformly continuous extension from a dense subspace")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No extension theorem for uniformly continuous maps, and no EXISTENCE or UNIQUENESS statement for an extension along a dense subspace. The library does have the metric completion (completion-is-metric-space, completion-is-complete, embed-isometry, embed-in-fun) and a density notion for sequences (is-dense-seq), but no theorem connects them to an extension; the only extends-on facts in the whole library are the Hahn-Banach ones (hahn-banach, hahn-banach-extend-one, hb-extend-construct).")
       (notes-statement "Suppose X, Y are metric spaces, A subset X is a dense subspace and Y is complete.  Then every uniformly continuous function f : A --> Y has a unique extension to a uniformly continuous function fbar : X --> Y."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.27")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "min(d,1) is a metric, with the same convergent sequences")
       (names trunc-metric-is-metric-space trunc-metric-dist trunc-metric-id-uniformly-continuous trunc-metric-id-uniformly-continuous-back bdd-metric-converges-iff)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The two halves are proven for DIFFERENT truncations. trunc-metric(s) is literally the notes' min(1, d) (trunc-metric-dist) and is proven to be a metric space, but for it the library offers only the uniform continuity of the identity in both directions, never the convergence equivalence. The convergence equivalence that IS proven, bdd-metric-converges-iff, is for bdd-metric(s) = d/(1 + d), which is not the notes' d'. So no single library object carries both conclusions of Proposition 3.27.")
       (notes-statement "The function d'(x,y) = min(d(x,y), 1) is a metric.  Convergence of a sequence relative to d' is equivalent to convergence relative to d."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.28")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Tychonov Theorem for Metric Spaces")
       (names compact-countable-product seq-compact-countable-product)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The product metric is FIXED by the library rather than chosen in the proof: product-metric(ms) is product-metric-w with the weight n |--> 1/2^(n+1) and, in each factor, the truncation bdd-metric = d/(1+d), NOT the notes' min(d, 1) with weights 2^-k. The countable family is presented as is-ms-sequence(ms), a function on nn, rather than as a countable family {X_k}. Compactness of every factor is the hypothesis forall n in nn, is-compact(ms(n)).")
       (notes-statement "The product of a countable family of compact metric spaces is compact."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.33")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "On a compact space pointwise and uniform convergence agree on an equicontinuous set")
       (names ptwise-cauchy-compact-equicont-unif unif-cauchy-cont-implies-uniform-limit uniform-limit-continuous)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The equivalence itself is not stated. The library proves only the CAUCHY form -- an equicontinuous family on a compact space that is pointwise Cauchy is uniformly Cauchy -- plus, separately, that a uniformly Cauchy family of continuous functions has a continuous uniform limit. Two further restrictions: the target is fixed to rr-ms, the family being typed fam in fun(nn, fun(pts(s), rr)), where the notes allow an arbitrary metric space Y; and the object is a SEQUENCE indexed by nn, where the notes speak of an arbitrary SET E of functions and its topology. The easy converse (uniform implies pointwise) is not stated either.")
       (notes-statement "If X is a compact metric space and E an equicontinuous set of functions X --> Y, then pointwise convergence of sequences in E is equivalent to uniform convergence."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.34")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Ascoli-Arzela")
       (names ascoli-arzela-sequential ascoli-sequential-from-diagonal ascoli-pointwise-diagonal)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "A SEQUENTIAL form, not the notes' relative compactness in the topology of uniform convergence: the library states that an equicontinuous, pointwise-bounded sequence of continuous functions on a compact X has a subsequence converging UNIFORMLY to a continuous limit. Four differences. (a) Y is fixed to rr-ms; the notes allow any compact metric Y. (b) pointwise-bounded(s, fam) is an added hypothesis, free in the notes because Y is compact. (c) X must be INHABITED -- forsome x, x in pts(s) -- an added guard against the empty metric space. (d) Only SUFFICIENCY is proven; the notes' necessity half, that a relatively compact set is equicontinuous, is absent, and with no relative-compactness notion in the library it cannot be stated.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose X, Y are compact metric spaces.  A necessary and sufficient condition that a set E of functions X --> Y be relatively compact in the topology of uniform convergence on X is that it be equicontinuous at every x in X."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.36")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Equicontinuous sequence converging on a dense set converges everywhere, with continuous limit")
       (names ascoli-dense-bridge equicont-dense-conv-implies-unif-cauchy equicont-dense-conv-ptwise-cauchy)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Y is fixed to rr-ms, so the notes' hypothesis that Y be a complete metric space is discharged by the completeness of RR rather than assumed; a general complete Y is not available. The dense set D is presented as a dense SEQUENCE is-dense-seq(s, dseq) rather than as a dense subset, and convergence on it as converges-on(s, fam, dseq). The library's conclusion is UNIFORM convergence to a continuous g, which is stronger than the notes' pointwise conclusion plus continuity of the limit. fam must be typed fam in fun(nn, fun(pts(s), rr)) and each fam(k) assumed is-continuous, where the notes assume only equicontinuity of the sequence.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose X is a compact metric space, Y a complete metric space and {f_k} an equicontinuous sequence of functions X --> Y.  If {f_k(x)} converges for x in some dense set D subset X, then f_k(x) --> f(x) for all x in X and the limit f is continuous."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.37")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The isometry group of a compact metric space is compact")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "is-isometry(s, t, f) is defined, but there is no group of isometric isomorphisms of a space, no sup metric d_Gamma(phi, psi) = sup_x d(phi(x), psi(x)) on such a set, and no compactness statement about either.")
       (notes-statement "If X is a compact metric space and Gamma is the group of isometric isomorphisms phi : X --> X with d_Gamma(phi, psi) = sup_{x in X} d_X(phi(x), psi(x)), then Gamma with d_Gamma is a compact metric space."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "3.38")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "In the isometry group uniform and pointwise convergence agree")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Absent, together with the group Gamma of Proposition 3.37 on which it is stated.")
       (notes-statement "Let Gamma be as in 3.37 and {g_i : i in N} a sequence of elements of Gamma.  Then g_i --> g uniformly if and only if g_i(x) --> g(x) for all x in X."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.5")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Regulated functions are exactly the uniform limits of step functions")
       (names uniform-limit-of-regulated continuous-is-regulated)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "NEITHER direction of the notes' equivalence is stated.  is-step-fn is defined in the library but NO theorem anywhere mentions it, so necessity (a regulated function is a uniform limit of step functions) is absent, and sufficiency is present only in the weakened form uniform-limit-of-regulated: a uniform limit of REGULATED functions is regulated. Separately, is-regulated(f, a, b) types f in fun(rr, rr), i.e. TOTAL on RR, with a and b as parameters, where the notes have f : [a,b] --> R; it demands a < b strictly; and it demands a right limit only where x_ < b and a left limit only where a < x_, where the notes (Remark 4.2) instead declare f(a-) = f(a) and f(b+) = f(b) so that all four one-sided limits exist.")
       (notes-statement "A necessary and sufficient condition that f : [a,b] --> R be regulated is that it be the uniform limit of step functions."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.8")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The antiderivable functions on [a,b] form a vector space")
       (names antiderivable-add antiderivable-scale antiderivable-sub)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Stated as three closure properties -- sum, scalar multiple and difference -- and not as a vector space: the library has no vector-space structure on a class of functions to name, so the conclusion of the notes has no counterpart. The functions are in fun(rr, rr), TOTAL on RR, with a and b as parameters, and is-antiderivable(phi, a, b) demands a < b strictly, so the degenerate interval is excluded. The sum is written as the explicit term vnb-lambda(x, rr, phi(x) + psi(x)).")
       (notes-statement "The set of antiderivable functions on [a,b] is a vector space."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.10")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Two antiderivatives of the same function differ by a constant")
       (names pw-antiderivative-differ-by-some-constant pw-antiderivative-differ-by-constant antiderivative-differ-by-constant)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-22 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name(s)), for the PIECEWISE primitive is-pw-antiderivative: continuous on [a, b], with derivative phi(t) at every t of (a, b) outside a finite set (Dieudonne 8.7 with a finite exceptional set; the notes' Definition 4.6 is the case of an empty exceptional set, which the library does not single out yet). The remaining names are the older forms for functions TOTAL on RR (is-antiderivative, c-int); they remain in the library.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are antiderivatives of phi on [a,b] then there is a constant C with f(s) = g(s) + C for all s in [a,b]."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.11")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The endpoint difference is the same for all antiderivatives")
       (names pw-antiderivative-endpoint-difference antiderivative-endpoint-difference)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-22 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name(s)), for the PIECEWISE primitive is-pw-antiderivative: continuous on [a, b], with derivative phi(t) at every t of (a, b) outside a finite set (Dieudonne 8.7 with a finite exceptional set; the notes' Definition 4.6 is the case of an empty exceptional set, which the library does not single out yet). The remaining names are the older forms for functions TOTAL on RR (is-antiderivative, c-int); they remain in the library.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are antiderivatives of phi on [a,b] then f(b) - f(a) = g(b) - g(a)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.12")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The definite integral is a linear functional")
       (names pw-antiderivative-sum pw-antiderivative-real-mul c-int-add c-int-scale)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-22 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name(s)), for the PIECEWISE primitive is-pw-antiderivative: continuous on [a, b], with derivative phi(t) at every t of (a, b) outside a finite set (Dieudonne 8.7 with a finite exceptional set; the notes' Definition 4.6 is the case of an empty exceptional set, which the library does not single out yet). `Linear functional' is given as two theorems, the sum and the real multiple: the space of antiderivable functions is not a structure in the library. The remaining names are the older forms for functions TOTAL on RR (is-antiderivative, c-int); they remain in the library.")
       (notes-statement "The definite integral is a linear functional on the space of antiderivable functions on [a,b]."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.13")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The integral of a sum is the sum of the integrals")
       (names pw-antiderivative-sum c-int-add antiderivative-add)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Restated 2026-09-22 for functions ON [a, b] (the first name(s)), for the PIECEWISE primitive is-pw-antiderivative: continuous on [a, b], with derivative phi(t) at every t of (a, b) outside a finite set (Dieudonne 8.7 with a finite exceptional set; the notes' Definition 4.6 is the case of an empty exceptional set, which the library does not single out yet). The remaining names are the older forms for functions TOTAL on RR (is-antiderivative, c-int); they remain in the library.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are antiderivatives of phi, psi respectively, then f + g is an antiderivative of phi + psi, and the integral of phi + psi over [a,b] is the sum of the two integrals."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.16")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Differentiating a sequence whose derivatives converge uniformly")
       (names antiderivative-family-uniform-limit antiderivable-uniform-limit diff-at-ptwise-limit)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The library version NORMALISES the sequence: it demands fam(k)(a) = 0 for EVERY k, where the notes assume only that {f_k(a)} is convergent, so the notes' hypothesis (2) is replaced by a stronger and non-equivalent one. Hypothesis (1) is replaced too: instead of f_k' being defined and CONTINUOUS on [a,b], the library assumes is-antiderivative(fam(k), phifam(k), a, b), which gives differentiability only on the OPEN interval (a,b) and says nothing about continuity of phifam(k). Of the four conclusions of the notes, it delivers the existence of f = lim f_k (as a uniform limit on ccint(a, b)) and is-antiderivative(f, psi, a, b); it does NOT state that psi is continuous, does not state that f is differentiable at the ENDPOINTS (the notes explicitly extend the conclusion to them via one-sided derivatives), and does not state f_k' --> f' uniformly. All functions are in fun(rr, rr), TOTAL on RR, uniform convergence is written inline over ccint(a, b), and a < b is strict.")
       (notes-statement "If {f_k} on [a,b] has each f_k' defined and continuous on [a,b], {f_k(a)} convergent, and f_k' --> psi uniformly on [a,b], then f(x) = lim f_k(x) exists for every x in [a,b], f is differentiable on [a,b], f' is continuous on [a,b] and f_k' --> f' uniformly."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "4.17")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The antiderivable functions are closed under uniform limits")
       (names antiderivable-uniform-limit)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Uniform convergence is spelled out inline (for every eps > 0 there is n_ in nn with abs(psi(x_) - (phifam(k_))(x_)) <= eps for all k_ >= n_ and all x_ in ccint(a, b)) rather than through a named predicate, and is required only on ccint(a, b). The sequence and its limit are in fun(rr, rr), TOTAL on RR, and a < b is strict, so the degenerate interval is excluded.")
       (notes-statement "The space of antiderivable functions on [a,b] is closed under uniform limits."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "5.2")
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Bernstein approximation of a continuous function on [0,1]")
       (names bernstein-uniform-approximation)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "f is required to be in fun(rr, rr), i.e. TOTAL on RR, with continuity assumed only at the points of ccint(0, 1) and there TWO-SIDED, including at 0 and at 1; the notes have f continuous on [0,1]. The conclusion is given as a single eps/N estimate -- eps is a PARAMETER of the theorem and the claim is forsome cap_ in nn, for all n_ >= cap_ and all x_ in ccint(0,1), abs(f_(x_) - bernstein-poly(f_, n_, x_)) <= eps_ -- rather than as the statement that f is the uniform limit of the sequence B_n. bernstein-poly(f, n, x) is the functoid series-partial-sum(l_ |--> f(l_/n) * (bernstein-basis(n,x))(l_), succ(n)), so the notes' sum from 0 to n appears as a partial sum of length n+1.")
       (notes-statement "Let f be a continuous function on [0,1].  Then f is the uniform limit of the Bernstein polynomials B_n(x) = sum_{k=0}^{n} f(k/n) B_{k,n}(x)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "5.4")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The two Bernstein estimates")
       (names bernstein-partition bernstein-variance-bound)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Both identities are stated with series-partial-sum(..., succ(n)) in place of the notes' sum from l = 0 to n, and bernstein-basis(n, x) is a function on ZZ (bernstein-basis-in-fun), so the out-of-range terms are zero by the separate bernstein-basis-above and bernstein-basis-null rather than by the range of the sum. The second estimate's summand cannot be written inline: it must be supplied as an auxiliary sequence h_ pinned pointwise by forall k_ in nn, h_(k_) = ((k_ - n_ x_)^2) (bernstein-basis(n_, x_))(k_). bernstein-variance-bound additionally requires n_ /= 0, which the notes leave implicit in the factor 1/n.")
       (notes-statement "The Bernstein polynomials satisfy sum_{l=0}^{n} B_{l,n}(x) = 1 and (1/n) sum_{l=0}^{n} (l - nx)^2 B_{l,n}(x) <= 1."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "5.5")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The four Bernstein identities")
       (names bernstein-partition bernstein-moment-1 bernstein-moment-2 bernstein-variance)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Each of the last three identities is stated through an auxiliary sequence h_ pinned pointwise (forall k_ in nn, h_(k_) = ...) and the sum is series-partial-sum(h_, succ(n_)), because the library has no inline finite-sum term with a bound variable; bernstein-basis(n, x) is a function on ZZ, so the range of the notes' sum is enforced by bernstein-basis-above and bernstein-basis-null rather than by the summation. The notes derive the identities from the Binomial Theorem by differentiating in x; the library's route is bernstein-basis-succ and induction, so the proof does not follow the notes (binomial-theorem exists independently).")
       (notes-statement "sum_l B_{l,n}(x) = 1; sum_l l B_{l,n}(x) = nx; sum_l l(l-1) B_{l,n}(x) = n(n-1)x^2; sum_l (l - nx)^2 B_{l,n}(x) = nx(1-x)."))

(entry (doc "calculus")
       (ref "5.7")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Weierstrass approximation on an arbitrary interval")
       (names bernstein-uniform-approximation-ccint)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The library exhibits ONE SPECIFIC approximant -- the transported Bernstein polynomial bernstein-poly(compose(fn_, x |--> a + (b-a)*x), n_, (x_ - a) * recip(b - a)) -- and bounds abs(fn_(x_) - that) by eps. It never says that this term IS a polynomial, and there is no statement anywhere of the form the notes make, that any continuous function on [a,b] is a uniform limit of a sequence of POLYNOMIALS: there is no predicate for being a polynomial function on RR to connect the two. Further: fn_ in fun(rr, rr), TOTAL on RR; continuity assumed two-sided at every x_ in ccint(a, b); a < b strictly, so the degenerate interval is excluded; and eps is a parameter with the claim in eps/N form rather than the notes' uniform-limit form.")
       (notes-statement "For any interval [a,b], any continuous function on [a,b] is a uniform limit of a sequence of polynomials."))

;;; ===== linear-algebra =====
;;; linear-algebra.sexp -- MAJOR THEOREMS of ~/docs/linear-algebra.tex, mapped to the
;;; VNB library.  Written by mapping agent MT-3, 2026-09-21.
;;;
;;; NUMBERING.  linear-algebra.tex is a standalone \chapter with no master document on
;;; this box, so the chapter number was not read off a preamble.  It is CHAPTER 3, with a
;;; single counter shared by dfn / lem / prop / cor / rem / example (the amsbook scheme of
;;; calculus.pdf).  Reconstruction confirmed independently at five points: the library's
;;; own reference/BY-TOPIC.md headings (Lemma 3.3, Prop 3.5, Cor 3.6, written by
;;; theorem-library/reference-topics.scm) and Claude's memory index (Cor 3.46 =
;;; cor:22195-45888-207248 at tex:1988, Prop 3.36 = row-reduction-of-matrices-over-er,
;;; Prop 3.41 = rank, Prop 3.44, Prop 3.48, Prop 3.50, Cor 3.51, Def 3.33, Rmk 3.35,
;;; Rmk 3.39) all agree with the ordinal count.  The \label follows each ref.
;;;
;;; Definitions (3.1, 3.2, 3.4, 3.7, 3.11, 3.16, 3.24, 3.28, 3.30, 3.33, 3.42), remarks
;;; (3.21, 3.32, 3.34, 3.35, 3.39) and examples (3.9, 3.14, 3.17) are NOT entries here.
;;; The lemma \iffalse'd out at tex:1651 carries no number and is not an entry.

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.3")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Action of the matrix unit E_n[k,l] on the right")
       (names matunit-col-shift)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Generality GAINED: the library proves it over an arbitrary ring (is-ring), while the notes' section fixes R commutative.  The library states the conclusion entrywise -- entry(matmul(a,p,matunit(a,n,k,l)),i,c) = if(c = l, entry(p,i,k), zero(a)) -- where the notes name the resulting matrix A'; same content.  No k /= l guard on either side.")
       (notes-statement "For any m x n matrix A, A E_n[k,l] = A' where A' is the m x n matrix whose l-th column is the k-th column of A and whose other entries are 0."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.5")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The elementary column operations")
       (names elem-f-action elem-g-action elem-h-action)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Generality GAINED: stated over an arbitrary ring (is-ring), not a commutative ring, and elem-h-action asks only r in carr(a), NOT that r be invertible (the notes build invertibility of r into the definition of H).  Split into three entrywise theorems on matmul(a,p,elem-x(...)) instead of one prose sentence; elem-f-action and elem-g-action carry the notes' own k /= l guard.  The row-operation mirrors (elem-f-row-action, elem-g-row-action, elem-h-row-action) are proven as well, which the notes only define by transposition.")
       (notes-statement "Right multiplication by F^col[k,l] interchanges columns k and l; by G^col[r,k,l] adds r times column k to column l; by H^col[r,k] multiplies column k by r."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.6")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The elementary column matrices are invertible, with explicit inverses")
       (names elem-f-inverse elem-g-inverse elem-h-inverse elem-f-invertible elem-g-invertible)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Two differences.  (1) F and G are given is-invertible-mat (elem-f-invertible, elem-g-invertible), but there is NO elem-h-invertible: for H the library installs only the product equation matmul(a,elem-h(a,n,r,k),elem-h(a,n,s,k)) = identmat(a,n) under the hypothesis mul(a)(r,s) = one(a), so the inverse is a SUPPLIED s, not r^{-1} -- the library has no ring-inverse operation.  (2) Generality gained: is-ring rather than a commutative ring.  The notes' G inverse formula G[-r,k,l] and the k /= l guard match elem-g-inverse exactly.")
       (notes-statement "Over a commutative ring R the matrices F^col[k,l], G^col[r,k,l] and (for r invertible) H^col[r,k] are invertible in M_n(R), with F^col[k,l]^{-1} = F^col[l,k], G^col[r,k,l]^{-1} = G^col[-r,k,l] for k /= l, and H^col[r,k]^{-1} = H^col[r^{-1},k]."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.8")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Column reduction of the first row over a field")
       (names clear-first-row class-min-pivot)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Three losses and one gain.  The library works over a EUCLIDEAN RING, not a field (gain), and therefore (a) cannot scale the pivot: clear-first-row concludes entry(q,1,1) = entry(p,1,1) and never normalises the corner to 0 or 1, which is precisely the notes' conclusion b_11 in {0,1}; (b) needs the pivot to be nonzero AND to MINIMISE the gauge over the whole equivalence class (that hypothesis is supplied by class-min-pivot), where the notes need no such thing; (c) works modulo MAT-EQUIV, which is two-sided (an invertible U on the left and V on the right), not the notes' column equivalence.  Extra guards: m, n in nn and 1 in interval(1,m), 1 in interval(1,n).")
       (notes-statement "Over a field R, any m x n matrix A is column equivalent to a B with b_{1j} = 0 for 2 <= j <= n and b_{11} either 0 or 1."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.10")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Column reduction to a step matrix with zeros above the diagonal")
       (names smith-diagonalization)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Neither statement implies the other.  smith-diagonalization reaches a FULLY DIAGONAL d (is-diagonal: every off-diagonal entry zero) over a euclidean ring, which is stronger than the notes' zeros above the diagonal; but it reaches it through MAT-EQUIV -- row AND column operations -- where the notes use COLUMN operations only, and it does not normalise the diagonal entries to 0 or 1 (that step needs a field).  The library has no column-only equivalence relation at all.")
       (notes-statement "Any m x n matrix A is column equivalent to a B with b_{ij} = 0 for 1 <= i < j <= n and each b_{ii} either 0 or 1, i.e. B has zero entries above the diagonal."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.12")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Over a field, invertible iff column equivalent to the identity")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing in the library relates invertibility to equivalence with the identity matrix, and there is no column-only equivalence to state it with.  Nearest: the definitions is-invertible-mat and mat-equiv, plus identmat-invertible and product-of-invertibles-is-invertible.")
       (notes-statement "If R is a field, A in M_n(R) is invertible if and only if A is column equivalent to the identity matrix."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.13")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The elementary column matrices generate the group of invertible matrices")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library never forms the group of invertible n x n matrices (mat-ring is a ring, and no unit group is carved out of it) and states no generation result for it.  Nearest: elem-f-invertible, elem-g-invertible, product-of-invertibles-is-invertible.")
       (notes-statement "If R is a field, the n x n elementary column matrices generate the group of invertible matrices in M_n(R)."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.15")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A permutation of the indeterminates is a ring automorphism of R[x_1,...,x_n]")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has the monoid algebra monalg(a,m) and, on top of it, poly(a) = monalg over the additive monoid of NN, i.e. ONE-VARIABLE polynomials only; there is no R[x_1,...,x_n].  permutations(n) is defined (injection(ord-segment(n), ord-segment(n))) but acts on nothing.  No sign of a permutation, hence none of the sign machinery under this proposition either.")
       (notes-statement "For a permutation sigma of the indeterminates, p |-> sigma . p is an isomorphism of the ring R[x_1, x_2, ..., x_n]."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.18")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "det is multilinear and alternating, and det of the transpose equals det")
       (names det-alternating-rows)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Only one of the three claims exists, and in a different form.  det-alternating-rows is ASSERTED (warrant reference) and says that two EQUAL rows force det = zero(r) -- not that interchanging two rows changes the sign.  Linearity in each row and in each column is absent.  det(A^t) = det(A) is absent and unstatable: the library has NO matrix transpose operation.  Ring hypothesis agrees (is-commutative-ring).  Note that the library's det is DEFINED by cofactor expansion along the first row, not by the notes' sum over permutations, so even the definitions are only provably equal in principle.")
       (notes-statement "det A is linear in each column and each row; interchanging two distinct rows or two distinct columns yields B with det A = - det B; and det(A^t) = det A."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.19")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Determinant under an elementary column operation")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The determinant of an elementary matrix is computed nowhere in the library -- there is no det of elem-f, elem-g or elem-h, and no det of a diagonal matrix from which det H could be read.  The asserted det-multiplicative would reduce these to those three values, which are missing.")
       (notes-statement "For A in M_n(R): det(A F^col[k,l]) = - det A = det A det F^col[k,l]; det(A G^col[r,k,l]) = det A = det A det G^col[r,k,l]; det(A H^col[r,k]) = r det A = det A det H^col[r,k]."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.20")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "det(AV) = det A det V for invertible V over a field")
       (names det-multiplicative)
       (status asserted)
       (deviation "The library asserts the UNRESTRICTED multiplicativity -- any commutative ring, any two square matrices of the same size, no invertibility and no field -- which subsumes this corollary.  It carries warrant reference, i.e. it is trusted, not proven.")
       (notes-statement "If R is a field and V in M_n(R) is invertible, then det(A V) = det A det V."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.22")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Expansion by minors along a row and along a column")
       (names det-cofactor)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The library DEFINES det by the cofactor expansion along the FIRST ROW: det-cofactor is the defining equation for det(r, succ(n), a), not a theorem.  So the notes' statement is available only at i = 1, and only as a definition; the expansion along an arbitrary row i, and the column expansion, are nowhere stated.  The library's minor(s,r,c,n) is the notes' A[i,j].  The sign (-1)^{i+j} appears in det-cofactor as mpow of neg(one(r)) to succ(j), the i = 1 specialisation.")
       (notes-statement "For each i, det A = sum_j (-1)^{i+j} a_{ij} det(A[i,j]); and for each j, det A = sum_i (-1)^{i+j} a_{ij} det(A[i,j])."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.23")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library defines is-diagonal (all off-diagonal entries zero) and border/submat, but computes no determinant from any of them, and has no notion of triangular matrix at all.")
       (notes-statement "If A is lower triangular then det A is the product of the diagonal entries; likewise for upper triangular A."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.25")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The formal adjoint satisfies adj(A) A = det(A) I")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No adjoint (adjugate) operator, no cofactor matrix, no Cramer rule.  minor and det exist separately; nothing assembles the cofactors into a matrix.")
       (notes-statement "For any n x n matrix A, the formal adjoint with entries (-1)^{i+j} det(A[j,i]) satisfies adj(A) A = det(A) I; hence A^{-1} = det(A)^{-1} adj(A)."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.26")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Over a field, invertible iff the determinant is nonzero")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing in the library connects is-invertible-mat with det in either direction.")
       (notes-statement "If R is a field, A in M_n(R) is invertible if and only if det A /= 0."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.27")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "det is multiplicative")
       (names det-multiplicative)
       (status asserted)
       (deviation "Same statement, same hypothesis (is-commutative-ring, both matrices n x n), but ASSERTED with warrant reference -- it is trusted surface, never proven.  It is one of only two determinant facts on the debt bundle (with det-alternating-rows).")
       (notes-statement "det(A B) = det A det B for all A, B in M_n(R), R a commutative ring."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.29")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Behaviour of the sigma-th order subdeterminants under F and G operations")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has no submatrix obtained by deleting SEVERAL rows and columns (minor deletes exactly one of each; submat and block cut a leading or trailing rectangle), no set minor_sigma A, and no sdet_sigma A.  Nothing about how determinants of such minors move under elementary operations.")
       (notes-statement "If B comes from A by an F row or column operation then sdet_sigma B = sdet_sigma A up to sign; if by a G operation then every element of sdet_sigma B has the form a + s a' with a, a' in sdet_sigma A and s = 0 or s = +/- r."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.31")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The gcd of the sigma-th subdeterminants is an invariant over Z")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Depends on 3.29, which is absent.  The library has zz-is-gcd and zz-bezout for a PAIR of integers, but no gcd of a set of integers and no subdeterminants.")
       (notes-statement "If A, B in M_{m x n}(Z) and A -> B by elementary F, G row and column operations over Z, then gcd sdet_sigma A = +/- gcd sdet_sigma B."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.36")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Integer normal form: existence and uniqueness of the diagonal form")
       (names smith-normal-form smith-diagonalization)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The heaviest gap in the chapter.  smith-normal-form gives, over a EUCLIDEAN RING (Z qualifies, zz-is-euclidean-ring), the EXISTENCE of a mat-equiv d that is is-diagonal and whose nonzero diagonal entries form an initial segment of length kk (smith-staircase).  It does NOT give the divisibility chain b_ii divides b_jj, does NOT give non-negativity of the entries, and does NOT give UNIQUENESS -- the notes' whole second half.  Moreover the notes' relation ~ is generated by integral elementary operations, whereas mat-equiv is d = U c V for SOME invertible U, V; the two are never shown to coincide.")
       (notes-statement "For A in M_{m x n}(Z) there is a UNIQUE B ~ A with (1) nonzero entries only on the diagonal, (2) all entries non-negative, (3) b_ii divides b_jj for j >= i."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.37")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The reducing matrices U and V are products of elementary matrices")
       (names smith-normal-form)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Unfolding mat-equiv inside smith-normal-form does produce invertible U (m x m) and V (n x n) with d = U A V, so part (1) holds as far as 3.36 goes.  Part (2) -- that U and V are PRODUCTS OF ELEMENTARY matrices -- is never established: mat-equiv is defined by existence of invertible factors, not by a sequence of elementary operations, and no theorem bridges the two.  The normal form reached also lacks 3.36's divisibility and non-negativity.")
       (notes-statement "For A in M_{m x n}(Z) there are U in M_m(Z), V in M_n(Z) with U A V in the normal form of 3.36, U a product of column elementary matrices and V a product of row elementary matrices; in particular both are invertible over Z."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.38")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "An invertible integer matrix is a product of elementary matrices")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No factorisation of an invertible matrix into elementary matrices anywhere in the library, in either direction.  The easy direction's ingredients exist (elem-f-invertible, elem-g-invertible, product-of-invertibles-is-invertible) but are never assembled.")
       (notes-statement "U in M_m(Z) is invertible over Z if and only if it is a product U V with U a product of column elementary matrices and V a product of row elementary matrices."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.40")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "An invertible change of sequence preserves generation and freeness")
       (names generates-transport free-transport spans-transport)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The library's two statements are ONE-WAY implications, not the notes' iff: generates(md,n,u) implies generates(md,n,matact(md,pm,u)), and rel-free(md,m,v) implies rel-free(md,m,matact(md,qm,v)).  The converses (which follow by applying the same theorems to the inverse matrix) are not installed.  Setting: a module md over any ring with the transport matrix invertible over scal(md); the notes' E is an ABELIAN GROUP and U is over Z, and the library has NO abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view (zz-act exists as an integer action, but no module structure is built from it), so the notes' own case is not an instance of the library statement.  spans-transport is the submodule version, not in the notes.")
       (notes-statement "If U in M_n^x(Z), then u = <u_1,...,u_n> generates E if and only if U . u generates E, and u is relation-free if and only if U . u is."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.41")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A relation-free sequence is no longer than a generating sequence")
       (names free-length-le-generators)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The first sentence is proven, for a module whose SCALAR RING IS EUCLIDEAN (the notes' Z case is only an instance once the missing abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view exists).  The second sentence -- that all free generating sequences of an abelian group have the same LENGTH, the fact that makes rank well defined -- is NOT stated anywhere, although it follows by two applications of the first.  Guards the notes do not carry: n, m in nn, and both sequences are column matrices u in mat(n,1,vec(md)), v in mat(m,1,vec(md)).")
       (notes-statement "If E is generated by n generators then any relation-free sequence has at most n elements; in particular all free generating sequences of an abelian group have the same length."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.43")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "GL_n(Z) acts freely and transitively on the free generating sequences")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Only the one-way transport exists (generates-transport, free-transport).  matact is never presented as a GROUP ACTION, and neither freeness nor transitivity of it is stated; the library does not form the group M_n^x(Z) at all.")
       (notes-statement "If E is a free abelian group of rank n, the action (A, u) |-> A . u of M_n^x(Z) on free generating sequences of E is free and transitive."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.44")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Adapted basis for a subgroup, with the divisibility chain")
       (names submodule-of-free-is-free)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Much weaker.  submodule-of-free-is-free (ASSERTED, warrant reference) gives only that the submodule has SOME family w of length k <= n that spans it and is rel-free.  It gives no ADAPTED BASIS e_1,...,e_n OF E, no coefficients b_i, hence no divisibility chain b_i divides b_{i+1}, no non-negativity, and no claim that the b_i are invariants of F.  Setting is a module over a euclidean ring rather than a free abelian group.")
       (notes-statement "If E is free abelian of rank n then for any subgroup F there are free generators e_1,...,e_n of E and non-negative integers b_1,...,b_n with b_i dividing b_{i+1} such that b_1 e_1, ..., b_n e_n generate F; the b_i are invariants of F."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.45")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The norm N_F(E): the absolute determinant is basis-independent")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No determinant of a change-of-basis matrix is ever taken, and no index or norm invariant of a submodule is defined.  Depends on 3.44's adapted basis and on determinant theory the library does not have.")
       (notes-statement "If F is contained in E, with free generating sets v, u and v = A . u, then |det A| does not depend on the choice of v and u."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.46")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A subgroup of a free abelian group of rank n is free of rank at most n")
       (names submodule-of-free-is-free)
       (status asserted)
       (deviation "ASSERTED with warrant reference -- trusted, not proven, and one of only 32 reference-tier supports in the library.  The library form is for a submodule sm of a module md over a EUCLIDEAN scalar ring, with a generating AND rel-free column family u of length n; the conclusion is a k <= n and a w in mat(k,1,vec(md)) with spans(md,k,w,sm) and rel-free(md,k,w) -- the notes' free of rank <= n, granted the missing abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view.  Its own harder input, 3.47, IS proven, so this is assembly debt, not mathematical debt.")
       (notes-statement "Any subgroup of a free abelian group of rank n is free of rank at most n."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.47")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A subgroup of an n-generated group is generated by at most n elements")
       (names submodule-fg spans-submodule-fg)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "This is the library's headline linear-algebra result, proven and billing modulo 0 since 2026-09-17.  Differences: it is stated for a MODULE over a euclidean ring (the notes' abelian group is not an instance, the abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view being absent), and its conclusion is a column family w in mat(k,1,vec(md)) with k <= n that SPANS the submodule.  spans-submodule-fg is the strictly more general form the descent actually needs: a submodule of a submodule spanned by n elements is spanned by k <= n.  The notes' own proof of this corollary (through Z^n and 3.44) is CIRCULAR -- Smith form consumes a finite generating set for F and cannot produce one -- so the library proves it directly, by descent on the ideal of last coefficients; the \\iffalse'd induction at tex:1651 is load-bearing in the notes.")
       (notes-statement "Any subgroup F of a group E generated by n elements is generated by a set of n elements or fewer."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.48")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of the principal sequence of a subgroup")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The uniqueness half of the Smith theory is nowhere in the library: smith-normal-form is pure existence, and neither the principal sequence nor the notion of invariant factor is defined.  It is exactly the part of 3.36 that the library dropped.")
       (notes-statement "The principal sequence b of a subgroup F of E is uniquely determined."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.49")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The quotient E/F for a diagonal subgroup")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has no quotient MODULE and no quotient group -- only the quotient of a SETOID (quotient, class, proj, quotient-universal) and ringoid-quotient-ring.  It also has no direct sum of modules and no cyclic group Z/(a).  is-hom-module-def exists as a homomorphism predicate, but nothing builds the map of this lemma.")
       (notes-statement "If E is freely generated by e_1,...,e_n, the a_1,...,a_k are non-zero and F is generated by a_1 e_1,...,a_k e_k, then E/F is isomorphic to Z/(a_1) + ... + Z/(a_k) + Z^{n-k}."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.50")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Structure of a finitely generated abelian group")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Absent for the reasons listed under 3.49 (no quotient module, no direct sum, no cyclic group) and under 3.48 (no uniqueness).  The nearest library assets are submodule-fg and smith-normal-form, which supply the existence half of the input but nothing of the conclusion.")
       (notes-statement "If E is a finitely generated abelian group there are w_1,...,w_l in E and non-negative a_1,...,a_l with a_i dividing a_j for i <= j such that (x_1,...,x_l) |-> sum x_i w_i induces an isomorphism Z/(a_1) + ... + Z/(a_l) -> E; the sequence a_1,...,a_l is unique up to sign."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.51")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Every finitely generated abelian group is a direct sum of cyclic groups")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Absent; it is the restatement of 3.50, and the library has neither the direct sum nor the cyclic groups nor the uniqueness.")
       (notes-statement "Every finitely generated abelian group is a finite direct sum Z/(m_1) + ... + Z/(m_l) with m_k > 0 and m_k dividing m_{k+1}; the representation is unique."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.52")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The cardinality of E/F is the norm N_E(F)")
       (names ())
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Depends on 3.45 (the norm) and 3.49 (the quotient), both absent.  CARD is defined in the library since 2026-09-20, but the quotient E/F it would be applied to does not exist.")
       (notes-statement "If E is a finitely generated free abelian group, F a subgroup and N_E(F) /= 0, then card(E/F) = N_E(F)."))

;;; ===== complex-analysis =====
;;; complex-analysis.sexp -- MAPPING agent MT-4, 2026-09-21.
;;; Document: /home/ubuntu/docs/complex-analysis.pdf, "Complex Analysis", F. Javier Thayer.
;;; Scope (by the user's decision): chapter 1 sections 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 1.6 (section 1.4
;;; SKIPPED), chapter 2 in full (2.1, 2.2, 2.3), and section 3.1 only.
;;; Numbered results of kind Remark or Definition are not listed (1.1, 1.4, 1.6, 1.8, 1.10,
;;; 1.13, 1.17, 1.23, 2.1, 2.6, 2.11); Prop 1.12 and Lemma 1.14 lie in the skipped 1.4.
;;; Library side read off reference/THEOREMS.md (proven / PSS / axiom / definition /
;;; assertion sections), reference/PSS.md, reference/DEFINITIONS.md; `proven' confirmed by
;;; grepping (qed 'NAME) under theorem-library/.

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.2")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of the least upper bound")
       (names esup-unique)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The library states uniqueness of the least upper bound only on the EXTENDED non-negative reals: esup-unique quantifies over s_ subset RR-POS-STAR with both candidate bounds in RR-POS-STAR. For a subset of RR the supremum is the primitive constant SUP, characterised by the three AXIOMS rr-sup-in / rr-sup-upper / rr-sup-least (warrant: reference), each guarded by non-emptiness of the set and boundedness above; uniqueness of a least upper bound of a subset of RR is nowhere stated.")
       (notes-statement "Any S subset of R has at most one least upper bound."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.3")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "C as 2x2 real matrices")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing about matrix representations of C. The nearest is the R-linear identification of C with R x R: cc-coords-is-bijection and cc-of-pair-is-bijection (proven), with cc-coords-add and cc-coords-real-mul; there is no set of 2x2 matrices of the form [[a,b],[-b,a]] and no statement that any map from C is an algebra (ring) isomorphism onto it.")
       (notes-statement "Phi : a + b i |-> [[a, b], [-b, a]] is an algebraic isomorphism from C onto the set of 2x2 real matrices of that form."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.5")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Unions and finite intersections of open sets")
       (names union-of-opens-open inter-of-opens-open)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The union half matches (union-of-opens-open: an indexed family g over an index set a, conclusion is-open(s, big-union(i, a, g(i)))). The intersection half is only BINARY: inter-of-opens-open takes two open sets u, w. The notes' claim for the intersection of FINITELY many open sets is not stated anywhere.")
       (notes-statement "The union of any family of open sets is open. The intersection of finitely many open sets is open."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.7")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Monotone convergence for real sequences")
       (names monotone-convergence-rr)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The library proves only that the sequence CONVERGES; it nowhere identifies the limit with sup_n x_n, which is the whole content of the notes' statement. Only the non-decreasing / bounded-above case is stated; the non-increasing / bounded-below mirror (lim = inf) is absent. Monotonicity is stated in the one-step form forall k in NN. f(k) <= f(succ(k)), and boundedness as forsome bnd in RR. forall k. f(k) <= bnd rather than through SUP.")
       (notes-statement "If {x_n} is non-decreasing and bounded above then lim_n x_n = sup_n x_n; if non-increasing and bounded below then lim_n x_n = inf_n x_n."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.9")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Closed sets and limits of sequences")
       (names closed-limit-in closed-set-limit-in)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Only the FORWARD direction is in the library (A closed implies every convergent sequence of elements of A has its limit in A), in two forms: closed-limit-in with f in fun(NN, a), and closed-set-limit-in with the pointwise membership forall n in NN. f(n) in a. The CONVERSE -- sequentially closed implies closed -- is not stated, so the notes' `if and only if' is not available, nor the corollary drawn from it in the notes (a set is closed iff it contains all its accumulation points).")
       (notes-statement "A set A in a metric space X is closed if and only if every sequence of elements of A which converges, converges to an element of A."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.11")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Characterisations of compactness")
       (names compact-iff-fip compact-iff-cluster-point compact-iff-tb-complete)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "All four conditions are present and each equivalence is proven, but as three separate IFF theorems against (1) rather than one four-way equivalence. In (2) the library's HAS-FIP carries two inhabitedness guards the notes do not: the family c must be non-empty (forsome a0. a0 in c), and only NON-EMPTY finite subfamilies are required to have a non-empty intersection. (3) is stated with CLUSTER-POINT defined by: for every eps > 0 and every m in NN there is n >= m with dist(f(n), x) < eps -- the notes define a cluster point by the set {n : x_n in B(y,r)} being infinite.")
       (notes-statement "For a metric space X the following are equivalent: (1) X is compact; (2) X satisfies the finite intersection property for closed sets; (3) every sequence in X has a cluster point; (4) X is totally bounded and complete."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.15")
       (kind theorem)
       (title "C is an algebraically closed field")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing about roots of polynomials over CC. The library has no polynomial with complex coefficients as a function on CC, no n-th roots of a complex number, and no statement that a non-constant polynomial has a zero. The nearest facts are the algebraic ones about CC: cc-is-normed-field (proven) -- note that IS-NORMED-FIELD has no MUL-INV slot and does NOT require multiplicative inverses, so it is not the field property -- together with the flat CC arithmetic axioms cc-recip-closed and cc-recip-inverse, which do supply inverses of non-zero complex numbers.")
       (notes-statement "C is an algebraically closed field: every polynomial equation a_n z^n + ... + a_0 = 0 with n >= 1 and a_n /= 0 has a solution in C."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.16")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Convergence in the extended plane")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has no one-point compactification of C: no C-hat, no topology on C union {infinity}, no |x_n| -> +infinity. Only convergence in the Euclidean metric space CC-MS is available (converges-to(cc-ms, f, l), with cc-converges-iff-coords and cc-complete), which is exactly the first half of the notes' `if and only if' but not the statement about the extended topology. The extended line RR-STAR / POS-INF exists but is a different object.")
       (notes-statement "For a sequence {x_n} in C: it converges to x in C in the topology of C-hat iff it converges to x in the Euclidean metric; it converges to infinity iff |x_n| -> +infinity."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.18")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Linear maps on C^2 and linear fractional transformations")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near. The library has no complex projective space P, no quotient of C^2 minus 0 by scalars, and no linear fractional transformations at all (grep for `projective', `fractional', `moebius' over THEOREMS.md returns nothing). C^2 itself is not a library object.")
       (notes-statement "An invertible complex linear map S on C^2 with matrix [[a,b],[c,d]] factors through a transformation L_S on the projective plane P; under the identification of P with C-hat, S |-> L_S is a surjective homomorphism from the group of invertible complex linear maps onto the set of linear fractional transformations, with kernel the scalar multiples of the identity."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.19")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The linear fractional transformations form a group")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no linear fractional transformations in the library.")
       (notes-statement "The set of linear fractional transformations is a group."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.20")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "SL(2,C) onto the linear fractional transformations")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no linear fractional transformations, no determinant-1 group of complex 2x2 matrices. The library's DET is for matrices over a commutative ring and is unconnected to any of this.")
       (notes-statement "S |-> L_S is a surjective homomorphism from the group of linear maps on C^2 with determinant 1 onto the set of linear fractional transformations, with kernel {-I, I}."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.21")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The Cayley transform")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no Cayley transform, no upper half plane, no unit circle T, no unit disc as a named object.")
       (notes-statement "The linear fractional transformation C z = (z - i)/(z + i) maps R bijectively onto T minus {1}, and maps the upper half plane H+ bijectively onto the open unit disc D."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.22")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The Hopf map is continuous and onto S^2")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no C^2, no spheres S^3 or S^2, no map pi(u1,u2) = (2 u1 conj(u2), |u1|^2 - |u2|^2). The library does have CONJUGATE and MAGNITUDE on CC (cc-conjugate-mul, cc-magnitude-mul, ...), the ingredients only.")
       (notes-statement "pi(u1, u2) = (2 u1 conj(u2), |u1|^2 - |u2|^2) is a continuous mapping C^2 -> C x R, and maps the sphere S^3 of C^2 onto the unit sphere S^2 of R^2 x R."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.24")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The Hopf fibration factors through a bijection S^2 -> P")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no projective plane, no spheres.")
       (notes-statement "The mapping pi factors through a bijection S^2 -> P."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.2")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Differentiable implies continuous")
       (names diff-on-implies-continuous)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for an arbitrary normed field K rather than for CC (more general; the CC instance is got by taking K = CC-NORMED-FIELD, but no CC-specialised corollary is installed). The conclusion is continuity at a in the SUBSPACE metric on U -- is-continuous-at(subspace-ms(nf-metric-space(k), u), nf-metric-space(k), f, a) -- not continuity of a map defined near a in C; the two agree because IS-DIFF-ON forces U open, but the library does not say so.")
       (notes-statement "If f : U -> C is differentiable at a in U (U open) then f is continuous at a."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.3")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Sum and product rules")
       (names diff-on-sum diff-on-product holomorphic-sum holomorphic-product)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Two separate developments, neither of which is exactly the notes' statement. diff-on-sum / diff-on-product give the derivative at a, but only for the LITERAL function VNB-LAMBDA x in U. (add K)(f(x), g(x)) (resp. the product); a pointwise-equal representative of f + g or f . g has to be transported by diff-on-transfer-ptwise-eq. holomorphic-sum / holomorphic-product do take an arbitrary sm in FUN(U, CC) that agrees pointwise, but they conclude only HOLOMORPHIC-ON(U, sm) and say nothing about the value of the derivative at a. Both diff-on laws are stated for a general normed field K.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are differentiable at a then f + g and f . g are differentiable at a, with [f+g]'(a) = f'(a) + g'(a) and [f.g]'(a) = f'(a) g(a) + f(a) g'(a)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.4")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Chain Rule")
       (names diff-on-chain)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The library adds hypotheses the notes leave implicit: both U and the target set S must be open (carried by IS-DIFF-ON), and forall y in U. f(y) in S is an explicit antecedent (the notes only require g to be differentiable at f(a)). The composite is the literal VNB-LAMBDA x in U. g(f(x)), so a pointwise-equal representative needs diff-on-transfer-ptwise-eq; the derivative is written (mul K)(m, l) in the normed field K. Stated for a general K, not specialised to CC.")
       (notes-statement "If f is differentiable at a and g is differentiable at f(a), then g o f is differentiable at a and (g o f)'(a) = g'(f(a)) f'(a)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.5")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Root Test")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No root test in any form. The library has no lim sup for real sequences: ELIMSUP / ELIMINF exist only for sequences into RR-POS-STAR = [0, +inf] and carry no theory beyond the definition of ECONVERGES-TO. The nearest convergence criteria for a non-negative real series are comparison-test (termwise domination by a convergent series) and geometric-series-converges; the divergence half has no analogue at all.")
       (notes-statement "For a sequence {theta_k} of non-negative reals with rho = lim sup_k theta_k^(1/k): the series sum_k theta_k converges if rho < 1 and diverges if rho > 1."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.7")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Radius of convergence by the root test")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The library has no notion of RADIUS OF CONVERGENCE at all, and no complex power series. Its power-series layer (structure-library/power-series.scm) is REAL and pointwise only: PS-PARTIAL-SUM(coef, x, k), PS-CONVERGES-AT(coef, x) and PS-ABSOLUTELY-CONVERGES-AT for coef in FUN(NN, RR) and x in RR, with the bridges ps-converges-as-series and ps-partial-sum-as-series. There is no lim sup of |a_k|^(1/k) to state the formula with.")
       (notes-statement "The radius of convergence of sum_k a_k z^k is 1 / lim sup_k |a_k|^(1/k)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.8")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Normal convergence inside the radius")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "No radius of convergence, no supremum norm on complex-valued functions, and no notion of normal convergence (the Weierstrass M-test is not in the library). The nearest machinery is uniform convergence of sequences of REAL functions on a metric space (CONVERGES-UNIFORMLY, unif-cauchy-has-uniform-limit, uniform-limit-continuous) and absolute convergence of a real series (series-abs-converges, ps-absolute-implies-convergent); none of it is applied to a power series on a closed ball.")
       (notes-statement "If R is the radius of convergence of sum_k a_k z^k then for every r < R the series is normally convergent on the closed ball B-bar(0,r); in particular it converges absolutely there and the convergence is uniform."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.9")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Polynomially bounded factors do not shrink the radius")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no radius of convergence, no comparison of two power series by their coefficients.")
       (notes-statement "If |b_k| <= C k^m for some C, m, then the radius of convergence of sum_k a_k z^k is at most that of sum_k a_k b_k z^k."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.10")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A power series is holomorphic and differentiates termwise")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near, in either the complex or the real case. The library's power series are real and pointwise (PS-CONVERGES-AT); it has no theorem that the sum function of a power series is differentiable, no termwise-differentiated series, and no statement that the two series share a radius. HOLOMORPHIC-ON is available (holomorphic-basics.scm) but the only holomorphic functions exhibited are constants, the identity, powers, sums and products (holomorphic-const, holomorphic-identity, holomorphic-power, holomorphic-sum, holomorphic-product).")
       (notes-statement "If sum_k a_k z^k has radius of convergence R then f(z) = sum_k a_k z^k is defined and holomorphic on B(0,R); the series g(z) = sum_{n>=1} n a_n z^(n-1) also has radius R, and f'(z) = g(z)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.12")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Principle of Analytic Continuation")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near. The library has no notion of an ANALYTIC function (no local power-series representation), and no connectedness of a subset of a metric space -- IS-CONNECTED does not exist in THEOREMS.md. Accumulation point is available only indirectly through CLOSURE / CLUSTER-POINT.")
       (notes-statement "Let U be a connected open set. If f is analytic in U and the set of zeros of f in U has an accumulation point in U, then f is identically 0 on U."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.13")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Finitely many zeros in a closed ball")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no analytic functions, no connectedness. CLOSED-BALL and card(...) in NN (finiteness) both exist, so only the statement is missing, not the vocabulary for its conclusion.")
       (notes-statement "Let U be a connected open set. If f is analytic in U and not identically 0, then f has only finitely many zeros within any closed ball B contained in U."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.14")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Taylor coefficients of an analytic function")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near in the complex case (no analytic functions, no radius of convergence). The nearest is the REAL elementary-calculus result the notes explicitly contrast this with: taylor-lagrange (proven) gives, for f in FUN(RR,RR) TOTAL on RR with a < x and TAYLOR-DIFFERENTIABLE(f, a, x, n), a theta in (a,x) with factorial(succ n) (f(x) - taylor-poly(f,a,n,x)) = f^(n+1)(theta) (x-a)^(n+1) -- a remainder bound for one n, with no claim of convergence of the partial sums.")
       (notes-statement "If f is analytic at a then the formal power series sum_k f^(k)(a) z^k has positive radius of convergence and f(z) = sum_k f^(k)(a)/k! (z-a)^k for z near a."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.15")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Ratio Test")
       (names ratio-test-converges)
       (status asserted)
       (deviation "A PSS entry (warrant: informal), not proven. The library's ratio test is not the notes' statement about a bare series of positive reals: it is about a REAL POWER SERIES, coef in FUN(NN, RR) with every coef(n) non-zero, and the ratio hypothesis is PS-RATIO-LIMIT(coef, l) -- the ratios |coef(n+1)/coef(n)| CONVERGE to l, a limit, not a lim sup -- with l in RR, 0 <= l; the conclusion is PS-CONVERGES-AT(coef, x) for every real x with |x| l < 1. The divergence half (lim sup of the ratios > 1 implies divergence) is absent.")
       (notes-statement "For a sequence {theta_k} of positive reals: if lim sup_k theta_(k+1)/theta_k < 1 then sum_k theta_k converges; if lim sup_k theta_(k+1)/theta_k > 1 it diverges."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.16")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The exponential series has infinite radius")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no radius of convergence, and the series sum_k z^k / k! is nowhere formed. FACTORIAL exists (factorial-real-pos, recip-factorial-in-rr) and is used inside the Taylor polynomial, but there is no theorem about the convergence of the exponential series, real or complex.")
       (notes-statement "The radius of convergence of sum_k z^k / k! is infinite."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.17")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "exp is a continuous homomorphism into C*")
       (names r-exp-add r-exp-continuous-at r-exp-pos)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "There is no complex exponential in the library. R-EXP is the REAL exponential, and it is not defined by the power series: LOG is defined as the oriented integral of 1/x from 1 (log-unfold) and R-EXP is its inverse, so the identification of R-EXP with the restriction of the notes' exp is not a library fact. What is proven is the real restriction of the statement: r-exp-add (exp(u+v) = exp(u) exp(v) for real u, v), r-exp-pos (0 < exp(y), i.e. the values land in the multiplicative group of the positive reals) and r-exp-continuous-at (continuity at each real point, in RR-MS). Nothing about C or C* = C minus {0}.")
       (notes-statement "z |-> exp z is a continuous homomorphism from the additive group of C into the multiplicative group C* = C minus {0}."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.18")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The kernel of exp is 2 pi i Z")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no complex exponential, no pi (the symbol pi occurs in THEOREMS.md only as a bound variable in pigeonhole-infinite), no trigonometric functions. The real fragment of the statement -- exp t = 1 for real t only when t = 0 -- follows from r-exp-strictly-increasing and r-exp-zero but is not itself installed.")
       (notes-statement "z |-> exp z takes the value 1 precisely when z is an integer multiple of 2 pi i."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.19")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "exp is a bijection on a horizontal strip")
       (names r-exp-log log-r-exp log-surjective r-exp-pos)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Only the real restriction, and under the library's own definition of R-EXP as the inverse of the integral LOG rather than as a power series. r-exp-log and log-r-exp say that R-EXP and LOG are mutually inverse between RR and the positive reals, log-surjective and r-exp-pos give surjectivity onto (0, +inf), and log-injective-pos / r-exp-strictly-increasing give injectivity. Nothing about the strips S = {-pi <= Im z < pi} or S' = {-pi < Im z < pi}, about C minus {0} or about C minus (-inf, 0]; there is no pi and no complex exponential to state them with.")
       (notes-statement "exp is a bijection from S = {z : -pi <= Im z < pi} onto C minus {0}, and a bijection from S' = {z : -pi < Im z < pi} onto C minus (-inf, 0]."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.20")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The logarithm series has radius 1")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: no radius of convergence, and the series sum_k (-1)^(k-1) z^k / k is nowhere formed. The library's derivative of the logarithm is log-deriv (is-diff-at of x |-> log(x) at c > 0 with value recip-star(c)), proved from the integral definition, not from a series.")
       (notes-statement "f(z) = sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k-1) z^k / k has radius of convergence 1, and f'(z) = (1 + z)^(-1)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.21")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The logarithm series agrees with ln on (0,2)")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Nothing near: the power series log_0 z = sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k-1) (z-1)^k / k does not exist in the library. LOG itself is present and is exactly the notes' ln (log-unfold: LOG(x) = the oriented integral of 1/z from 1 to x, with log-one, log-mul, log-deriv, log-strictly-increasing proven), so only the series and its identification with LOG are missing.")
       (notes-statement "The function log_0 defined by the series sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k-1) (z-1)^k / k coincides on the open interval (0,2) with the natural logarithm ln on the positive reals."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.1")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Path additivity of the line integral")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Not in the library. Since 2026-09-21 the DEFINITIONS it needs exist, for functions ON [a, b] (structure-library/path-integral.scm): is-path, is-road(gamma, dgamma, a, b) with the derivative dgamma an explicit argument, trace, cc-int (the notes' equation (44)) and line-int(f, gamma, dgamma, a, b) = cc-int(t |-> f(gamma(t)) * dgamma(t), a, b); the segment t |-> z0 + t * w on [0, 1] is proven to be a road. The integral pw-int is defined by a description whose uniqueness obligation (two piecewise primitives of one function have the same endpoint difference) is NOT yet proven, so no value of a line integral can be computed yet. Juxtaposition of roads is not defined yet.")
       (notes-statement "If gamma, rho are paths such that the concatenation gamma || rho is defined, then the integral of f over gamma || rho is the integral over gamma plus the integral over rho."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.2")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Fundamental theorem for line integrals")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Not in the library. Since 2026-09-21 the DEFINITIONS it needs exist, for functions ON [a, b] (structure-library/path-integral.scm): is-path, is-road(gamma, dgamma, a, b) with the derivative dgamma an explicit argument, trace, cc-int (the notes' equation (44)) and line-int(f, gamma, dgamma, a, b) = cc-int(t |-> f(gamma(t)) * dgamma(t), a, b); the segment t |-> z0 + t * w on [0, 1] is proven to be a road. The integral pw-int is defined by a description whose uniqueness obligation (two piecewise primitives of one function have the same endpoint difference) is NOT yet proven, so no value of a line integral can be computed yet.")
       (notes-statement "If f is holomorphic in the open set U and gamma : [alpha,beta] -> U is a path, then the integral of f' along gamma is f(gamma(beta)) - f(gamma(alpha))."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.3")
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Polynomials integrate to zero over a closed path")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Not in the library. Since 2026-09-21 the DEFINITIONS it needs exist, for functions ON [a, b] (structure-library/path-integral.scm): is-path, is-road(gamma, dgamma, a, b) with the derivative dgamma an explicit argument, trace, cc-int (the notes' equation (44)) and line-int(f, gamma, dgamma, a, b) = cc-int(t |-> f(gamma(t)) * dgamma(t), a, b); the segment t |-> z0 + t * w on [0, 1] is proven to be a road. The integral pw-int is defined by a description whose uniqueness obligation (two piecewise primitives of one function have the same endpoint difference) is NOT yet proven, so no value of a line integral can be computed yet.")
       (notes-statement "If f is a polynomial function and gamma is a closed path, then the integral of f along gamma is 0."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.4")
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Splitting a segment integral at an interior point")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Not in the library. Since 2026-09-21 the DEFINITIONS it needs exist, for functions ON [a, b] (structure-library/path-integral.scm): is-path, is-road(gamma, dgamma, a, b) with the derivative dgamma an explicit argument, trace, cc-int (the notes' equation (44)) and line-int(f, gamma, dgamma, a, b) = cc-int(t |-> f(gamma(t)) * dgamma(t), a, b); the segment t |-> z0 + t * w on [0, 1] is proven to be a road. The integral pw-int is defined by a description whose uniqueness obligation (two piecewise primitives of one function have the same endpoint difference) is NOT yet proven, so no value of a line integral can be computed yet.")
       (notes-statement "If c is an element of the segment <a,b>, then the integral of f along <a,b> is the integral along <a,c> plus the integral along <c,b>."))
